The documentation viewer's helper binary lives here, as `mo`.

It is NOT in this repository. `release.yml` builds it from source, signs it, and
asserts its architecture and its Host-header check before the app bundle is
made; a local build simply has no viewer, which is a supported state — the Docs
tab lists, filters, reads and reveals every document without one, and only the
"open in the browser" button needs it.

This file is committed, and it is load-bearing. `tauri.conf.json` bundles
`viewer/*`, and a glob that matches NOTHING is a hard build failure ("glob
pattern viewer/* path not found or didn't match any files") — so without a file
that is always here, every local `tauri build` would break in order to ship a
binary that local builds do not have. The map form of `resources` fails the same
way for the same reason. Measured, not assumed.

What the app does with it: `app/src-tauri/src/viewer.rs`. Why it is a patched
build rather than a release binary: `docs/proposals/place-docs.md` §13-§14 — the
stock viewer does not validate the Host header, so a loopback bind does not keep
out the browser on this machine. The app re-proves that check on every spawn and
refuses to hand out a URL if it fails, so a binary that arrived here by some
other route cannot quietly serve the user's documents to a web page.
